--TEST--
the local tier forgets an older entry rather than failing the write that crossed `[cache.local] max_size` — `rule:core-api/two-cache-tiers` and `rule:concurrency/cache-memory-is-charged-to-the-core`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// § 3 caps this tier and says exceeding the cap **evicts** rather than failing
// an allocation, so nothing here is wrapped in a `try`: a `put` that has to
// make room answers exactly what a `put` with room to spare answers, and this
// case fails on an uncaught throw the moment that stops being true.
var $cache = Core\Cache::local();
var $payload = Core\Str::repeat('x', 1024);

$cache->put('first', $payload);
$cache->put('second', $payload);
// Three kilobyte entries do not fit under a three-kilobyte cap, so writing the
// third forgets the one written longest ago — § 1's "any entry may be absent at
// any time", reached by the one mechanism that decides which entry it is.
$cache->put('third', $payload);

// `null` is an ordinary answer here and never a failure, which is the whole
// difference between forgetting an entry and refusing a write.
echo $cache->get('first') == null ? 'forgotten' : 'kept', "\n";
echo $cache->get('second') == null ? 'forgotten' : 'kept', "\n";
echo $cache->get('third') == null ? 'forgotten' : 'kept', "\n";
--FILE nvs.toml--
# The cap is a `System` directive an operator sets, and nothing in the program
# above can raise it for itself: the memory it bounds belongs to the core, not
# to this request.
[cache.local]
max_size = "3K"
--EXPECT--
forgotten
kept
kept
